Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
i'm will yakowitz a staff writer at  forbes magazine where i cover cannabis  al harrington is a former nba player  played for the the denver nuggets the  new york knicks the indiana pacers al  harrington started a cannabis company  named viola and it operates in  california michigan  washington and oregon i also cut a  licensing deal in canada and he's going  to expand it to a couple other states  throughout the u.s viola has raised  about 36 million dollars and its  valuation is roughly 100 million last  year the company brought in 20 million  dollars in revenue when i got into 2011  i was just trying to figure out what the  hell i was doing and how i was going to  even start a business but it took me  about three years in  when i started to you know  do more research about the history of  cannabis on the war on drug side of  things when i realized that  you know 85 percent of all drug arrests  and black communities have been cannabis  related it just really opened my eyes  that like you know we cannot let this  industry continue to move forward  without proper representation and  diversity you know in the space i  started working with a group here in l.a  called ggc that you know enlightened me  to a lot of you know things that i just  didn't know  and you know once i started to get that  knowledge i realized that i needed to  change kind of our course of you know  that we were going  um and it wasn't all about just fighting  the stigma but it was also you know  trying to figure out how we can right  the wrongs you know and use our company  as a platform to actually do that one of  the most important aspects of viola is  that al harrington is african-american  and that's a big deal because in the  cannabis industry which generated 25  billion dollars in sales last year only  two percent of companies are black owned  al harrington wants to grow viola's  business he wants to increase the  footprint to 15 states within two years  but he does have a grander vision he  launched an incubator program which he  plans to use to help create 100 black  millionaires in the cannabis space his  ultimate goal with incubator is to  increase equity and ownership and  diversity in the cannabis industry you  know i think that the main reason why a  lot of people especially people of color  miss out on unbelievable opportunities  because it's just a lack of  communication and it's really just about  you know educating our people and giving  our people um access to knowledge  which will obviously i think will  eventually turn into opportunities you  know we try to do it through employment  um we try to do it through our incubator  program you know we just really just try  to reach out and just try to have an  impact as best that we can  one of the companies harrington has been  incubating his gold standard farms which  is a hemp farm in milton tennessee gold  standard has a really fascinating and  rich history so the current ceo jarrell  howard his great grandfather started the  farm in 1941. jerrell howard's great  grandfather was a sharecropper who  descended from enslaved people in  mississippi gold standard started as a  soybean and corn farm and for 80 years  they've been  judging along having a profitable  business but not making great money in  2018 the price of soybeans crashed in  the u.s jerrell howard needed to figure  out his next cash crop to sustain the  family business so that same year the  farm bill passed which legalized  industrial hemp and so when he was  thinking about  the next crop to use he started kind of  doing numbers right and he said okay soy  gets about 600 an acre but hemp could  fetch as much as 10 grand an acre so the  economics were a no-brainer so jarrell  had to pitch his company's board of  directors which are all related to him  you know eight elders ages 50 to 90. he  proposed doing hemp and they said yes  but you need a mentor and he found one  in al harrington the big problem was  that he didn't know him he started out  messaging him on linkedin there was no  response so he planted some hemp did a  harvest and then he started crossing  different strains and he came up with  what is now his signature product which  is purple hemp viola's logo is also  purple so he sent a sample to harrington  and he and he you know got back right  away harrington was really impressed and  was really impressed with the fact that  jerrell howard pivoted his family  business into hemp from soybeans and  they started working together  the transformation of gold standard  farms business has been dramatic over  the last 40 years the farm made nine  hundred thousand dollars in revenue  after it pivoted to hemp gerald howard  says he expects the company to make  three million dollars this year it just  goes to show you like you know this  cannabis is is definitely a huge  opportunity and you know we just got to  figure out ways once again that some  make it something out of nothing thing  this is a farm that's been 40 acres  that's been sitting there for 80 years  and you know obviously barely making it  essentially right to now you know being  able to transfer to him and look what  happened and i think there's a lot of  other opportunities out there like that  and that's what i just try to make  myself available to those opportunities  another method harrington is using to  mint cannabis millionaires is through  his other company called village llc and  that company pursues licenses in  certain states to grow sell manufacture  cannabis and these licenses are  inherently valuable especially in states  like  illinois maryland where the number of  licenses the state has decided to give  out are limited these licenses are  valued at three five ten million dollars  depending on the state depending on the  location that you get you cannot operate  a cannabis business without one of these  licenses and if you're in a state that  has a cap on the number of licenses  they're going to give out that drives up  the price what harrington has been doing  is um he'll find an entrepreneur in in a  state that he wants to start a business  and and partner with them our biggest  thing is just trying to get as many  people as we can to be able to take  advantage of the opportunity in the  right way that's why i think it's so  important that our people get some of  these licenses because we know how to  have the proper impact we know what  initiatives to push and different things  like that you know we have that know-how  so that's why once again it's so  important that you know we have a seat  at the table one of the entrepreneurs  that harrington has helped become a  millionaire through this licensing  process is this guy abe givens givens is  from missouri so when that state was  opening its medical marijuana licensing  process he told harrington that they  should apply how they structured it was  that it would be abe givens his license  he would launch a business in  partnership with harrington and given's  cousin who's larry hughes another former  nba player  they ended up getting a license and now  they're gonna open two dispensaries and  one cultivation site it's very tough you  know working with startups right because  like i said you know cash is you know  tough to hard to come by right now and  the industry goes up and down one minute  is all kind of cash available i mean  it's it's not  so it makes it tough but you know for me  i just i try to invest in people you  know i got to believe in the story i got  to believe in you you got to sell me so  far i feel like i've done a pretty good  job of that because everybody that we  pretty much bought in you know we still  continue to work with and we're just  just trying to grow you know brands  wanted one you know one brand at a time  black americans are almost four times as  likely to get arrested for a cannabis  offense than white people even though  usage rates are the same  so one of the big motivators for for  harrington is the impact that the war on  drugs had on people of color in america  he grew up being profiled and searched  by the police he told me this story that  you know almost every afternoon during  the school week him and his friends  after you know playing ball would go to  the corner store and hang out every  afternoon sure enough three to four  police cars would come and tell  everybody to get against the wall and  turn their pockets inside out and they'd  be searched and these are children these  are nine-year-old children it was scary  you know what i'm saying just not  knowing i don't know if they're going to  lock you up it was [ __ ] up but you  know what i mean it's just it just kind  of came with it that's just that's just  how that's just what happens it lingers  i mean i'm 42 years old obviously a  millionaire multi-millionaire living  nice houses all everything the nice cars  everything but still when the police  pulled me over i still i'm scared  because i don't know how it's going to  end up  as he started his cannabis business and  he started you know talking to people  about the war on drugs he realized that  like that's that's his experience and he  realized it's his own community's  experience and so this is a major  motivator for why harrington wants to  create a hundred black millionaires in  the cannabis space and increase  diversity and inclusion in the industry  how can  this drug have done so much harm in our  communities and now is this new billion  dollar industry and  not only are we not in position to you  know  participate but we're still locked up  behind it cannabis is still being police  the same way in our communities and i  think that that's another reason why you  know as the industry does grow when we  think about trying to create businesses  cannabis businesses within our  communities there's so much  uh fight back i always say like our  communities just have this ptsd they  don't they just can't understand that  like this is a real opportunity where  you know generational wealth  can come from this and we understand  that this fight is going to be a fight  that's going to be forever you know what  i mean i don't think that i'm ever going  to wake up one morning and say job well  done when we finally got there you know  i think this is going to be an issue  that's going to be around for a very  very long time but i think the more that  we continue to put it on the forefront  and make it an issue i think the change  will eventually happen  [Music]

